# ADGM Court Procedure Rules 2016, Rule 68 — summary judgment

Source: ADGM Court Procedure Rules 2016, Rule 68 (Grounds for Summary
Judgment). Pinned URL in `adgm_cpr_summary_judgment_source.yaml`. In
force; current.

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md): ADGM Court Procedure Rules
2016, © Abu Dhabi Global Market. Reproduced from the ADGM-published
PDF for academic / research purposes.

NOTE: this module was previously labelled "Rule 24" by analogy with
the English CPR; in the ADGM CPR 2016 the operative rule number is
**Rule 68**. The encoding's logic and tests are unchanged — only the
citation is corrected here.

## Rule 68 — Grounds for summary judgment (verbatim)

Reproduced verbatim from the ADGM Court Procedure Rules 2016
(consolidated 30 November 2023):

68. Grounds for summary judgment

(1) The Court may give summary judgment against a claimant or defendant
on the whole of a claim or on a particular issue if it considers that —

  (a) the claimant has no real prospect of succeeding on the claim or
      issue; or

  (b) the defendant has no real prospect of successfully defending the
      claim or issue; and

  (c) there is no other compelling reason why the case or issue should
      be disposed of at trial.

(2) An application for summary judgment may be made in accordance with
the provisions in Part 8.

(3) A claimant may not apply for summary judgment until the defendant
against whom summary judgment is sought has filed an acknowledgment of
service unless the Court gives permission, or a rule or relevant
practice direction provides otherwise.

(4) Paragraph (3) does not apply to a claim filed in the Small Claims
Division to which Part 37 applies, to a claim filed in the Employment
Division to which Part 38 applies, or to a claim filed in the Real
Property Division to which Part 41 applies.

(5) If a claimant applies for summary judgment before a defendant
against whom the application is made has filed a defence, that
defendant need not file a defence before the summary judgment hearing.

The "no real prospect" limb is read against the *Easyair* gloss
(*Easyair Ltd v Opal Telecom Ltd* [2009] EWHC 339 (Ch), per Lewison J):
the prospect must be more than fanciful, but the court must avoid
conducting a mini-trial. Limbs (a)/(b) and (c) are conjunctive.

## Encoding

```catala
declaration structure SummaryJudgmentApplication:
  data no_realistic_prospect content boolean
  data no_compelling_reason content boolean

declaration structure SummaryJudgmentDisposition:
  data both_limbs_made_out content boolean
  data summary_judgment_granted content boolean

declaration scope SummaryJudgmentTest:
  input application content SummaryJudgmentApplication
  output disposition content SummaryJudgmentDisposition

scope SummaryJudgmentTest:
  definition disposition equals
    let both equals
      application.no_realistic_prospect
      and application.no_compelling_reason
    in
    SummaryJudgmentDisposition {
      -- both_limbs_made_out: both
      -- summary_judgment_granted: both
    }
```

## Application

ADGM CFI applies Rule 68 across the contract-dispute docket for early
disposal. Structurally parallel to DIFC RDC Part 24 (DIFC's
summary-judgment rule, which is itself patterned on English CPR Pt 24
and applies the identical conjunctive two-limb test).

Applies in:
  ADGM contract disputes; default-judgment contests; jurisdictional
  summary applications.

## Tests

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestSummaryJudgmentBothLimbs:
  composite scope SummaryJudgmentTest
  output disposition content SummaryJudgmentDisposition

scope TestSummaryJudgmentBothLimbs:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  definition composite.application equals
    SummaryJudgmentApplication {
      -- no_realistic_prospect: true
      -- no_compelling_reason: true
    }
  assertion disposition.summary_judgment_granted = true

#[test] declaration scope TestSummaryJudgmentRefusedOnCompellingReason:
  composite scope SummaryJudgmentTest
  output disposition content SummaryJudgmentDisposition

scope TestSummaryJudgmentRefusedOnCompellingReason:
  definition disposition equals composite.disposition
  ## Even where the prospect limb is made out, a compelling reason for
  ## trial (e.g. credibility issues that need cross-examination) is
  ## sufficient to refuse summary judgment.
  definition composite.application equals
    SummaryJudgmentApplication {
      -- no_realistic_prospect: true
      -- no_compelling_reason: false
    }
  assertion disposition.summary_judgment_granted = false
```
